        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <B>Objectives:</B><P>Myoblast cells have been widely employed in conventional studies of biological processes because characteristics of intact muscle can be readily observed in these cultured cells. The purpose of this experiment was to investigate the effects of spaceflight on muscle by utilizing a well characterized myoblast cell line cultured in the Space Tissue Loss flight module. More specifically, this study aims to: 1) determine the role or microgravity in regulating the proliferation and differentiation of various skeletal muscle myoblast cell lines, 2) determine whether phenotypic changes are the direct result of microgravity-modulated gene expression.<P><B>Approach:</B><P>Cultures of L8 myoblast cells were monitored and prepared for loading into ground control and flight cartridges. Ground/Flight media bags were prepared without added growth factors, and once they were determined to be satisfactory, without contamination, additional growth components (L-gluatamine, chick embryo extract and antibiotics) were added. During flight, cells were monitored for growth, contamination, and fusing. Postflight, cultures were labeled in order to evaluate cell proliferation and differentiation. The cultures were analyzed on days 1, 2, 4, 6 and 8 for sarcomeric myosin, a- and §-actin, desmin, vimentin, and titin immunofluorescent antibody staining patterns. Furthermore, the expression of genes related to myogenesis (MyoD, MRF4, myf-5, myogenin, and ID) were analyzed with blotting and Northern hybridization analysis.<P><B>Results:</B><P>Cell nuclei labeling revealed that while microgravity does decrease the ability of myoblast cells to differentiate into myofibrils, it does not seems to affect their natural ability to cease proliferation when cultured to confluence in vitro. Both sarcomeric myosin and a-sarcomeric actin are actively expressed after L8 cells fuse to form myotubes.  Little of either protein is seen prior to fusion. Spaceflown cells, however, fuse to a much lesser extent than do ground-based cultures. §-actin has an opposite expression schedule.  Expression is dramatically reduced within 2 days after confluence, regardless of whether fusion occurs. Therefore, it appears that while spaceflight significantly decreases the ability of L8 cells to fuse and differentiate into myotubes, the expression and assembly of sarcomeric myosin, a-sarcomeric actin, and §-actin filaments remain unchanged in control cells and in those few cells spaceflown cells which progress to the fusing stage. However, more importantly, in those spaceflown cells that do not fuse, neither sarcomeric myosin nor a-sarcomeric actin filaments are detected.
        </div>

